            "complex_modifications": {
                "rules": [
                    {
                        "description": "ergoemacs motion: left cmd + i/k/j/l arrows, u/o by word. Everywhere but emacs, which does the same split itself through ns-command-modifier -- terminals included, which is the point. A real arrow is what a TUI reads; ESC+letter is what only a readline-style line editor reads. less, htop and fzf scroll on arrows and know nothing of ESC+i, and in Claude Code the prompt history and cursor motion are handled inside its text input as raw key events, with no bindable action behind them at all. Option+arrow survives the trip: Claude Code bundles node's readline keypress parser, which sets meta from either a leading ESC or a CSI modifier parameter, so \\e[1;3D and \\e\\e[D both arrive as meta+left and move by word. What does NOT survive is cmd+arrow -- node has no super modifier and a terminal does not encode cmd -- so line ends go through Home/End in the terminal rule below rather than through this one. The cost of all this is that ESC+i/k/j/l/u/o no longer exist in a terminal: left option + i is taken by the space-switch rule and ghostty's macos-option-as-alt=left rules out right option. .zshrc therefore binds the arrow sequences to the same widgets the ESC+letter bindings have, and keeps the latter for linux, where Alt is Meta directly.",
