                    {
                        "description": "ergoemacs deletion: left cmd + d/f delete a char back/forward, e/r a word back/forward. d, f and e reach terminals too, on the same reasoning as the motion rule: backspace, delete and option+backspace are real keys, and zsh, readline and Claude Code all already do the right thing with them -- \\x7f, \\e[3~ and \\e\\x7f respectively. Before this they went out as ESC+d/f/e, where Claude Code's hardcoded meta table reads ESC d as kill-word-FORWARD and ESC f as forward-word, so left cmd + d used to eat the word ahead of the cursor instead of the character behind it. r is the one left out: option+delete would be \\e[3;3~, which Claude Code reads as meta+delete and treats as kill-to-end-of-line rather than kill-word, so r keeps going through the terminal rule and does nothing there. The only sequence Claude Code would accept for kill-word is ESC d, and .zshrc binds that to backward-delete-char for the linux side.",
                        "manipulators": [
