                    {
                        "description": "left option + m switches apps: a quicker reach than cmd+tab, and left option was free -- it only composed a mu. Not scoped to any app, so it works everywhere. Indistinguishable from cmd+tab in use, the held switcher included: karabiner keeps left_command down for as long as m is held, so the window stays up and further presses walk it. The terminal tab rule above emits exactly this trigger, left option + m, and does not set it off: karabiner posts its output to the virtual keyboard without feeding it back through the rules, so the two coexist and left cmd + m still switches tabs in a terminal rather than switching apps. Tested, not assumed -- it is the kind of collision that would otherwise be silent.",
                        "manipulators": [
                            {
                                "from": {
                                    "key_code": "m",
                                    "modifiers": { "mandatory": ["left_option"] }
                                },
                                "to": [
                                    {
                                        "key_code": "tab",
                                        "modifiers": ["left_command"]
                                    }
                                ],
                                "type": "basic"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "description": "left option + u and i switch spaces, left and right. They send mission control's ctrl+left and ctrl+right, which System Settings has enabled -- turn those off there and this rule goes quiet with nothing to show why. Global, like the app switch on option+m. The cost is the dead keys: option+u composed an umlaut and option+i a circumflex, and karabiner takes them before any app sees them, emacs included. In a terminal, physical option+u used to arrive as ESC u for backward-word; that now switches spaces, and backward-word stays on left cmd + u, which is the intended gesture anyway.",
                        "manipulators": [
                            {
                                "from": {
                                    "key_code": "u",
                                    "modifiers": { "mandatory": ["left_option"] }
                                },
                                "to": [
                                    {
                                        "key_code": "left_arrow",
                                        "modifiers": ["left_control"]
                                    }
                                ],
                                "type": "basic"
                            },
                            {
                                "from": {
                                    "key_code": "i",
                                    "modifiers": { "mandatory": ["left_option"] }
                                },
                                "to": [
                                    {
                                        "key_code": "right_arrow",
                                        "modifiers": ["left_control"]
                                    }
                                ],
                                "type": "basic"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "description": "App launchers on right option: f firefox, v vscode, g ghostty, t telegram, r emacs. open brings an app forward when it is already running rather than starting a second. One manipulator per app, so each costs only that letter's composed character -- emacs is on r rather than e to leave the dead keys alone, option + e i u n and backtick being the accents on a US layout. Deliberately not a Hyper layer: binding right option to ctrl+option+shift+cmd would spend the whole key, where a binding per letter costs only that letter's composed character -- right option keeps composing everything else and stays free for other uses. Absolute path to open because karabiner runs shell_command with a bare environment, and the bundle id rather than the name so a rename cannot break it.",
                        "manipulators": [
                            {
                                "from": {
                                    "key_code": "f",
                                    "modifiers": { "mandatory": ["right_option"] }
                                },
                                "to": [{ "shell_command": "/usr/bin/open -b org.mozilla.firefox" }],
                                "type": "basic"
                            },
                            {
